--TEST--
What counts as one member of a `Core\ObjectSet` is `==`'s own table, one row per representation: the numeric domain is one row, a string and an array compare by content, and an object only with itself — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 9, `rule:expressions/equality-semantics` and `rule:expressions/object-identity-equality`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 9 says these collections "key on identity", and `rule:expressions/equality-semantics`'s table is
// what identity means — `nvs_runtime::identity` answers `==`, `Core\Arr::
// contains` and this store from one comparison, so a set's membership is
// exactly the equality the rest of the language already has.

// `int`, `uint` and `float` are one numeric domain, so all three spellings of
// one are one member.
Core\ObjectSet<mixed> $values = new Core\ObjectSet<mixed>();
$values->add(1);
$values->add(1 as uint);
$values->add(1.0);
echo $values->count(), "\n";

// `==` never converts, so the string, the bool and `null` are three further
// members rather than three more spellings of that one.
$values->add("1");
$values->add(true);
$values->add(null);
echo $values->count(), " ", $values->has(null) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// A string compares by content and never by pointer, so a string built at run
// time is the member a literal already put there.
Core\ObjectSet<string> $names = new Core\ObjectSet<string>();
$names->add("ab");
$names->add(Core\Str::join(["a", "b"], ""));
$names->add(Core\Str::lower("AB"));
echo $names->count(), "\n";

// An array compares entry by entry, in order — so two equal literals are one
// member and a reordering is another.
Core\ObjectSet<array<int>> $rows = new Core\ObjectSet<array<int>>();
$rows->add([1, 2]);
$rows->add([1, 2]);
$rows->add([2, 1]);
echo $rows->count(), " ", $rows->has([1, 2]) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// An object is identical only to itself, whatever it holds: `rule:expressions/object-identity-equality`
// leaves content equality to `Comparable::compareTo`, so three tags with one
// name are three members and each keeps its own value.
final class Tag {
    public function constructor(public readonly string $name) {}
}

array<Tag> $tags = [new Tag("x"), new Tag("x"), new Tag("x")];
Core\ObjectMap<Tag, int> $map = new Core\ObjectMap<Tag, int>();
int $i = 0;
foreach ($tags as Tag $tag) {
    $map->set($tag, $i);
    $i = $i + 1;
}
echo $map->count(), " ", $map->get($tags["1"]) ?? -1, " ", $map->get(new Tag("x")) ?? -1, "\n";

// A callable is an object, so two identical anonymous functions are two members and
// a copy of one callable value is not a second.
Core\ObjectSet<callable> $callbacks = new Core\ObjectSet<callable>();
callable $double = fn(int $n): int => $n * 2;
callable $alias = $double;
$callbacks->add($double);
$callbacks->add($alias);
$callbacks->add(fn(int $n): int => $n * 2);
echo $callbacks->count(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
1
4 yes
1
2 yes
3 1 -1
2
